  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 305 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nThe ridge to our west starts sliding east on Saturday, with a\nwarm frontal passage across the area late Saturday night or \nSunday. Ensembles are in good agreement that temperatures will\nexceed normal values and remain above normal for several days,\nwith a blocking high setting up over the southeast U.S. early in\nthe week and remaining there through at least Thursday. Extreme Forecast\nIndex (EFI) values for max temps remain at 0.85-0.95 (as high \nas 0.97, even), with Shift of Tails values generally below 1 \nwith some localized areas where Shift of Tails values reach \naround 1, suggesting high confidence in anomalous temperatures \nbut not necessarily an extreme event. High temperatures climb to\nupper 90s and even triple digits on Sunday, and stay in that\n96-100 degree range through the end of the forecast period next\nThursday. \n\nAdditionally, the return to southerly low-level flow will advect\nmoisture back into the region, though the highest PWATs appear\nto lag behind the warm front by 12-24 hours. Heat indices break\ntriple digits in the far western reaches of the area on Sunday,\nbut once moisture spreads across the area Monday, widespread \n100+ degree heat indices can be expected and will persist \nthrough much of the week. As such, Heat Risk increases to Major \n(Level 3 of 4, affecting anyone without effective cooling and/or\nadequate hydration) on Sunday and remains there through \nWednesday. Pockets of Extreme Heat Risk (Level 4 of 4, rare \nand/or long- duration extreme heat with little to no overnight \nrelief affecting anyone without effective cooling and/or \nhydration) begin to develop on Monday, with the Extreme Risk \nareas expanding Tuesday and Wednesday. Generally, the areas that\nwill see highest temperatures, and thus highest heat indices \nand overall risk, are to the west and north of the plateau. \n\nAs a shortwave trough pushes across the northern Plains this\nweekend, the pressure gradient across the Midwest tightens,\nincreasing winds to 10-15 mph with gusts up to 25 mph west of\nHighway 65 on Saturday and Sunday. RHs decrease to around 30% in\nthe afternoon both days this weekend, which could result in some\nareas with locally elevated fire weather conditions, especially\nwith today's Drought Monitor update indicating that drought has\nexpanded northward.",
